\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{Conjugate characters on two lines}
\date{2026-11-26}

\p{Let a generator #{g} of a cyclic group of order three act
on #{V_+=\mathbb C} by a primitive cube root #{\zeta} and on
#{V_-=\mathbb C} by #{\zeta^{-1}}. With the orthogonal sum
metric, #{(v,w)\mapsto(\bar w,\bar v)} is an antiunitary
[equivariant sector exchange](fdip-018A). Conjugation sends
#{\zeta} to #{\zeta^{-1}} and vice versa, so it intertwines
both actions. A linear map from the first line to the second
would have to satisfy #{\zeta u(v)=\zeta^{-1}u(v)} to
intertwine them. Since the two roots differ, that restriction
must vanish.}
